const body = `<h1>Compound Assignment &amp; Range of Variables</h1>

<p>
    Two short topics that the exam loves because both hide in plain sight. The
    first is shorthand you already use. The second is the discovery that a number
    type is a box with walls, and Java will not tell you when you hit them.
</p>

<h2>The Compound Family</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Shorthand</th><th>Long form</th><th>In the game</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>x += 5</code></td><td><code>x = x + 5</code></td><td>loot gold</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>x -= 5</code></td><td><code>x = x - 5</code></td><td>take damage</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>x *= 2</code></td><td><code>x = x * 2</code></td><td>double gold, powerups</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>x /= 2</code></td><td><code>x = x / 2</code></td><td>halve a stack, curses</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>x %= 10</code></td><td><code>x = x % 10</code></td><td>last digit, wraparound counters</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>x++</code></td><td><code>x = x + 1</code></td><td>loop counters, level up</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>x--</code></td><td><code>x = x - 1</code></td><td>ammo, cooldowns</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The whole right side is computed first, then combined. <code>hp -= damage * 2</code>
means <code>hp = hp - (damage * 2)</code>, not <code>(hp - damage) * 2</code>.</p>

<h2>Pre and Post Increment</h2>

<p><code>x++</code> and <code>++x</code> both add 1. The difference only appears
when the value is USED at the same moment it changes:</p>

<pre><code>int x = 5;
int a = x++;   // a gets 5, THEN x becomes 6   (post: use old value, then add)
System.out.println(a + " " + x);   // 5 6

int y = 5;
int b = ++y;   // y becomes 6 FIRST, then b gets 6   (pre: add, then use)
System.out.println(b + " " + y);   // 6 6</code></pre>

<p>In a plain statement, <code>x++;</code> alone, the two are identical. The exam
creates the difference by embedding the increment in an assignment or a print.</p>

<h2>Range: Every Type Has Walls</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Smallest</th><th>Largest</th><th>Game use</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>int</code></td><td><code>Integer.MIN_VALUE</code><br>(-2,147,483,648)</td>
        <td><code>Integer.MAX_VALUE</code><br>(2,147,483,647)</td><td>HP, gold, scores</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>double</code></td><td>about -1.8 x 10^308</td><td>about 1.8 x 10^308</td>
        <td>rates, ratios, exact-enough math</td></tr>
</table>

<p>An int counts from about negative 2.1 billion to positive 2.1 billion. That
sounds infinite until you double things.</p>

<h2>Overflow: The Silent Wraparound</h2>

<pre><code>System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE);      // 2147483647
System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE + 1); // -2147483648
System.out.println(Integer.MIN_VALUE - 1); // 2147483647</code></pre>

<p>Past the top, ints wrap to the bottom. Past the bottom, they wrap to the top.
No exception. No warning. The odometer rolled over. Any arithmetic that can cross
2.1 billion silently produces wrong answers from that point on.</p>

<div class="callout trap">
    <strong>When this actually bites:</strong> doubling loops (1 to 2 billion is
    only 31 doublings), factorials (13! overflows an int), and accumulating totals
    across a long campaign (1 billion damage in a year of play is a rounding error
    away). If a counter can double, or a total can grow without bound, ask: "will
    this pass 2.1 billion?" If maybe, widen to double (or long, which is an even
    bigger int box, though the CED scope uses int and double).
</div>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: Find the Wall</h2>

<div class="tinker">
    <h3>The Doubling Room</h3>
    <pre><code>int gold = 1;
int rooms = 0;
while (gold &gt; 0) {
    gold *= 2;
    rooms++;
    System.out.println(rooms + ": " + gold);
}</code></pre>
    <p>Run it. The loop exits not when gold gets huge, but when it goes NEGATIVE
    (overflow wrapped it). Count the rooms. Now compute by hand: 2 to the 30th is
    1,073,741,824. 2 to the 31st is 2,147,483,648, one past MAX_VALUE. The wall is
    at room 31. The loop ends at room 32, when the wrap makes gold negative. The
    program "works" the whole time. That is the horror and the fun.</p>
</div>

<h2>Socratic Checkpoint</h2>

<div class="socratic">
    <h3>Why doesn't Java just stop with an error at overflow?</h3>
    <p>Speed. Integer arithmetic at hardware speed means no range checks on every
    operation. Java's designers chose fast-and-silent over safe-and-slow, and put
    the responsibility on the programmer. Knowing when to check is part of knowing
    how to program. (Some languages do check. There is a real tradeoff here, and
    it is a great thing to argue about.)</p>
    <h3>Why is 13! the first factorial that overflows?</h3>
    <p>12! is 479,001,600, comfortably inside 2.1 billion. 13! is 6,227,020,800,
    past the wall. Compute both with a loop and watch 12! print correctly and 13!
    print garbage. Then write one sentence: "n! overflows an int at n = 13 because
    ___."</p>
</div>

<h2>Bug Hunt</h2>

<div class="bug-hunt">
    <h3>Bug 1: The Loot Bonus</h3>
    <pre><code>int totalGold = 2_000_000_000;   // campaign total so far
int bonus = totalGold + 500_000_000;
System.out.println("Bonus total: " + bonus);</code></pre>
    <p>Predict, then run. The total crosses 2.1 billion. What prints? What SHOULD
    print? Fix it with double and note what changes about the output formatting.</p>
    <h3>Bug 2: The Post-Increment Confusion</h3>
    <pre><code>int level = 3;
System.out.println("Level up! Now level " + level++);
System.out.println("Level check: " + level);
System.out.println("Doubled: " + ++level * 2);</code></pre>
    <p>Predict all three lines, then run. Line 1 prints 3 (old value used, level
    becomes 4 after). Line 2 prints 4. Line 3: level becomes 5 first, then times 2,
    so 10. If any line surprised you, write the rule in your notes.</p>
    <h3>Bug 3: The Halving Loop</h3>
    <pre><code>int stack = 100;
while (stack != 0) {
    stack /= 2;
}</code></pre>
    <p>This one is about integer division, not range. Trace it: 100, 50, 25, 12,
    6, 3, 1, then 1/2 is 0. Loop ends. Fine. Now change the divisor to 3. Trace:
    100, 33, 11, 3, 1, 1/3 is 0. Also fine. So when does this loop hang? (When
    stack starts at 0: 0/2 is 0 forever. Off-by-one on the start condition, the
    oldest bug in computing.)</p>
</div>

